  <body>
    <h1>
      <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>sannidhikāraka</b></mark><b>sikkhāpadaṃ  </b> 
    </h1>
    <br/>
    <ul>
        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>background</b></mark> 
            <ul>
                <li>Buddha in Jtvn; V. Bellaṭṭhasīsa (V. Ānanda&#39;s teacher) brought <b>sukkhakura</b> and had novices dry it, store it, and offer it to him when he needed; he went piṇḍapāta only once every few weeks; Buddha reprimanded him and laid down the rule</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>description</b></mark> 
            <ul>
                <li>what should be done with <b>surplus </b>yāvakālika and yāmakālika?
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>nirāpekkhadāna</b>: give it away to anupa without expectation of getting it back </li>
                        <li>or <b>anapekkhavissajjana</b>: relinquish the paṭiggahaṇa without expectation</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>sannidhikāraka</b></mark> / <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>sannidhikāra</b></mark>↔a yāvakālika or yāmakālika that was accepted and kept overnight
“<b>Sannidhikārameva sannidhikārakaṃ</b>. <b>Paṭiggahetvā ekarattaṃ vītināmitassetaṃ adhivacanaṃ”</b>.
                    <ul>
                        <li>collecting a sannidhikāraka item intending to consume it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{8356032867337719::<b>dkṭ</b>}}</mark><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>
</b></mark>consuming it:{{8356032867337719:: }}<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{8356032867337719::<b>pācittiya for every swallow</b>}}</mark><b> </b> 
                            <ul>
                                <li>pcpāḷi: <b>Khādissāmi bhuñjissāmīti paṭiggaṇhāti </b>(collecting)<b> āpatti dukkaṭassa</b>
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>here, paṭiggaṇhāti means collecting, since it is already accepted</li>
                                        <li><b>Sannidhikārakanti kāro karaṇaṃ kiriyāti atthato ekaṃ, sannidhi kāro assāti sannidhikāraṃ,  sannidhikārameva sannidhikārakaṃ, paṭiggahetvā ekarattiṃ vītināmitassetaṃ nāmaṃ.  Tasmā evaṃ sannidhikataṃ yaṃkiñci yāvakālikaṃ vā yāmakālikaṃ vā ‘Ajjhoharissāmī’ti  gaṇhantassa paṭiggahaṇe dukkaṭaṃ, ajjhohāre ajjhohāre pācittiyaṃ. (Kṅk. 225.).   </b> </li>
                                        <li><b>Paṭiggaṇhāti āpatti dukkaṭassāti evaṃ sannidhikataṃ yaṃkiñci yāvakālikaṃ vā yāmakālikaṃ  vā ajjhoharitukāmatāya gaṇhantassa paṭiggahaṇe tāva āpatti dukkaṭassa. (V-a. IV. 104.).   </b> </li>
                                        <li><b>Paṭiggahaṇeti gahaṇameva sandhāya vuttaṃ. Paṭiggahitameva hi taṃ, puna  paṭiggahaṇakiccaṃ natthi. Teneva &quot;ajjhoharitukāmatāya gaṇhantassa paṭiggahaṇe&quot;ti  vuttaṃ. Mātikāṭṭhakathāyaṃ (kaṅkhā. Aṭṭha. Sannidhikārakasikkhāpadavaṇṇanā) pana  &quot;ajjhoharissāmīti gaṇhantassa paṭiggahaṇe&quot;icceva vuttaṃ. (Srd-ṭ. III. 67.).   </b> </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>how to <b>prevent</b> an accepted item from becoming sannidhi?
                            <ul>
                                <li>its <b>paṭiggahaṇa nature</b> (the nature of having been properly accepted) must be abandoned by either <b>pariccajana </b>(nirāpekkhadāna) or anapekkha<b>vissajjana</b> (relinquishing without expectation of getting it back)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>paṭiggahaṇa</b></mark>
                    <ul>
                        <li>the nature of having been properly accepted with kāya or kāyapaṭibaddha</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>paṭiggahita</b></mark>
                    <ul>
                        <li>the edible that has been properly accepted with kāya or kāyapaṭibaddha (and other conditions as well)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>appaṭiggahita</b></mark>
                    <ul>
                        <li>the edible that has not been properly accepted with kāya or kāyapaṭibaddha; it can be one&#39;s belonging or not</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>pariccajana</b></mark>
                    <ul>
                        <li>the giving away of the yāva- or yāma-kālika to an anupa  </li>
                        <li>2 types of <b>pariccajana </b>(dāna)
                            <ul>
                                <li><b>sāpekkha</b>dāna: giving it to an anupa with the expectation of getting it back‒it does not abandon the paṭiggahaṇa
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>yāva- or yāma-kālika is given away with sāpekkhadāna and is stored over night: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{46546982289729755::<b>sannidhi, because the paṭiggahaṇa was not removed</b>}}</mark> 

receiving such item from an anupa the following day intending to eat it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{46546982289729755::<b>dkṭ</b>}}</mark>
consuming it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{46546982289729755::<b>pc for every swallow</b>}}</mark> </li>
                                        <li>can arahats store food for future use?
                                            <ul>
                                                <li>no, it is not possible; V. Bellaṭṭhasīsa gave it away, but expecting to consume it when needed</li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li><b>nirapekkha</b>dāna:<b> </b>giving it to an anupa without expectation of getting it back‒it does abandon the paṭiggahaṇa
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>receiving an edible that was sannidhi and given away with nirāpekkhadāna: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{7470049222540873::<b>anāpatti</b>}}</mark>
consuming it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{7470049222540873::<b>anāpatti</b>}}</mark> 

giving nirāpekkhadāna knowing one might / will get it back: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{7470049222540873::<b>no problem, it is still nirāpekkha</b>}}</mark> 

if expectation arises after doing the dāna: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{7470049222540873::<b>no problem, it is still nirāpekkha</b>}}</mark></li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>vissajjana</b></mark>↔the relinquishing of the paṭiggahaṇa of a yāva- or yāma-kālika
                    <ul>
                        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>anapekkha</b></mark>vissajjana
                            <ul>
                                <li>the mental giving up of the paṭiggahaṇa of the yāma- or yāva-kālika, intending not to consume it based on the present paṭiggahaṇa</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>doing anapekkhavissajjana, receiving the yāma- or yāva-kālika on a later occasion, and consuming it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{23619551850130516::<b>anāpatti (srtdpn)</b>}}</mark> 
                            <ul>
                                <li><b>Yaṃ paṭiggahetvā apariccattameva hi dutiyadivase na vaṭṭatīti ettha paṭiggahaṇe  anapekkhavissajjanena anupasampannassa nirapekkhadānena vā vijahitapaṭiggahaṇaṃ pariccattameva hotīti &quot;apariccatta&quot;nti iminā ubhayathāpi avijahitapaṭiggahaṇameva vuttaṃ.  Tasmā yaṃ parassa pariccajitvā adinnampi sace paṭiggahaṇe nirapekkhavissajjanena  vijahitapaṭiggahaṇaṃ hoti, tampi dutiyadivase vaṭṭatīti veditabbaṃ. (Srd-ṭ. III. 67.).</b> </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>does <b>anapekkhavissajjana</b> suffice to avoid sannidhi?
                            <ul>
                                <li>srtdpn: yes, there is no sannidhi and it can be consumed on another day after being offered</li>
                                <li><b>note:</b>
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>vmtvndn: no</li>
                                        <li>some Myanmar teachers do not like srtdpn&#39;s idea</li>
                                        <li>it avoids sannidhi, but for it to be consumed the next day, it has to be nirapekkhadāna based on the fact that when one gives the bowl to an anupa to wash, if food is left in the bowl and gets mixed with next day&#39;s food, it is a <b>pc</b> 
                                            <ul>
                                                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>whose idea is this?</i></mark><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li>if food is left in the bowl, mixed with next day&#39;s food, and one consumes it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{06537727766755208::<b>pc</b>}}</mark> </li>
                                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>is giving the bowl to an anupa to wash considered anapekkhavissajjana or nirāpekkhadāna? </i></mark> #<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>pariccatta </b></mark>/<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b> vijahitapaṭiggahaṇa</b></mark>↔yāma- or yāva-kālika of which the paṭiggahaṇa was relinquished‒either by nirapekkhadāna or anapekkhavissajjana (srtdpn-ṭ)</li>
                        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>apariccatta</b></mark> / <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>avijahitapaṭiggahaṇa</b></mark>↔yāma- or yāva-kālika of which the paṭiggahaṇa was <u><b>not</b></u><b> </b>relinquished
                            <ul>
                                <li><b>Sayaṃ paṭiggahetvā apariccattameva hi dutiyadivase na vaṭṭatīti ettha paṭiggahaṇe anapekkhavissajjanena anupasampannassa nirapekkhadānena vā vijahitapaṭiggahaṇaṃ pariccattameva hotīti &quot;apariccatta&quot;nti iminā ubhayathāpi avijahitapaṭiggahaṇameva vuttaṃ.</b> </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><b>note:</b> the words <b>pariccajana</b> and <b>vissajjana</b> are interchangeable; here, it follows sāratthadīpanī&#39;s usage</li>
                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>santaka appaṭiggahita </b></mark>(unaccepted belonging)↔food that belongs to a monk, but is not paṭiggahita (not properly accepted with kāya or kāyapaṭibaddha)
                    <ul>
                        <li>when one is offered food that can be kept for a long time, how can one make it a santaka appaṭiggahita?
                            <ul>
                                <li>the monk should hand it over to an anupa without touching it; it can then be properly accepted and consumed on later days</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>can a monk have santaka appaṭiggahita food <b>moved, given away, etc</b>?
                            <ul>
                                <li>yes, but not by touching it</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>does santaka appaṭiggahita become <b>sannidhi</b>?
                            <ul>
                                <li>no, because it does not have the paṭiggahaṇa nature</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>antovuttha</b></mark>↔santaka appaṭiggahita yāma- or yāva-kālika that was stored overnight in a vāsāgāra of a bhikkhu(s), or of the saṅgha 
                            <ul>
                                <li>consuming <b>antovuttha: </b><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{34772449574465747::<b>dkṭ for every swallow</b>}}</mark> 

if santaka appaṭiggahita is stored overnight in an unallowable place: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{34772449574465747::<b>antovuttha</b>}}</mark>

if the ownership is given to anupa and it is stored in an unallowable place: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{34772449574465747::<b>not antovuttha</b>}}</mark> </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>anivāsageha</b></mark>↔a building that was not built as a dwelling place; e.g.: uposathāgāra 
                            <ul>
                                <li><b>Vihāraṃ ṭhapetvāti upasampannānaṃ vāsatthāya katagehaṃ ṭhapetvāti attho. Gehanti  nivāsagehaṃ,  tadaññaṃ  pana  uposathāgārādi  sabbaṃ  anivāsagehaṃ  catukappiyabhūmivimuttā pañcamī kappiyabhūmi. Saṅghasantakepi hi etādise gehe suṭṭhu  parikkhittārāmattepi abbhokāse viya antovutthādidoso natthi. Yena kenaci channe,  paricchanne ca sahaseyyappahonake bhikkhusaṅghassa nivāsagehe antovutthādidoso, na  aññattha. Tenāha &quot;yaṃ panā&quot;tiādi. (Vmv-ṭ. II. 190.).   </b> </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><b>2 ways</b> to deal with santaka appaṭiggahita
                            <ul>
                                <li>to <b>keep</b> the ownership, but have an anupa store it in an <b>allowable</b> place (kappiyabhūmi)</li>
                                <li>to <b>give</b> the ownership to an anupa; the anupa can store it <b>anywhere  </b> 
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>is it ok to do so having the <b>expectation</b> of getting it later?
                                            <ul>
                                                <li>yes, because the monk&#39;s ownership is broken, and the item belongs to the anupa, whose properties do not become antovuttha</li>
                                                <li>this looks like sāpekkhadāna, but here the item is appaṭiggahita
                                                    <ul>
                                                        <li>if the item were paṭiggahita and one gave it away with sāpekkha, the paṭiggahaṇa would not be broken and it would become sannidhi; but here, the item is appaṭiggahita, so a sāpekkhadāna works</li>
                                                    </ul>
                                                </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                        <li>giving an <b>antovuttha </b>as a sāpekkhadāna, receiving it back and consuming it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{8558829205695961::<b>it loses the antovuttha status and can be consumed</b>}}</mark> 

giving a <b>paṭiggahita </b>yāva- or yāma-kālika on the following day (sannidhi) as a sāpekkhadāna, receiving it back and consuming it: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{8558829205695961::<b>āpatti</b>}}</mark> 
                                            <ul>
                                                <li><b>Anupasampannassa dātabbo</b> <b>assātiādinā akappiyakuṭiyaṃ vutthampi</b> (when kept in an akappiyakuti) <b>anupasampannassa dinne</b> (when given to an anupa) <b>kappiyaṃ hoti</b> (becomes allowable), <b>sāpekkhadānañcettha vaṭṭati</b> (can give with expectation), <b>paṭiggahaṇaṃ viya na hotīti dasseti</b> (it is not like paṭiggahaṇa). (Vmv-ṭ. II. 190.).    </li>
                                                <li><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>which āpatti?</i></mark><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><b>bhūmis</b> in which santaka appaṭiggahita can be stored in (1 akappiya and 5 kappiya)
                            <ul>
                                <li>1 <b>akappiya</b>bhūmi (prohibited buildings)
                                    <ul>
                                        <li><b>vāsāgāra</b> (dwelling place) of a bhikkhu(s) / the saṅgha: becomes <b>antovuttha</b> </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                                <li>5 <b>kappiya</b>bhūmi (allowed buildings) 
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>saṅghika / puggalika <b>anivāsageha</b> (vmtvndn-ṭ): a building that was not build as a dwelling place for a monk(s), or the saṅgha </li>
                                        <li><b>ussāvanantikā: </b>a building made as a kappiyakuṭi to store food by a vinayakamma in which the placement of the first stone / pillar has to be done simultaneously with the recital of the sentence</li>
                                        <li><b>gonisādikā: </b>a kuṭi in an aparikkhitta monastery (not bounded)
                                            <ul>
                                                <li><b>go</b> from cow: not allowing cows to enter</li>
                                                <li><b>note: </b>the kuṭi being unprotected is better in this case, to discourage the storing of food</li>
                                                <li><b>gonisādikā duvidhā – ārāmagonisādikā, vihāragonisādikāti. tāsu yattha neva ārāmo na senāsanāni parikkhittāni honti, ayaṃ &quot;ārāmagonisādikā&quot; nāma. yattha senāsanāni sabbāni vā ekaccāni vā parikkhittāni, ārāmo aparikkhitto, ayaṃ &quot;vihāragonisādikā&quot; nāma. </b> </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                        <li><b>gahapati </b>(5 types) 
                                            <ul>
                                                <li>a dwelling belonging to a <b>householder</b> </li>
                                                <li>a dwelling built or being built for monks / saṅgha, but <b>still unoffered</b>, and still belonging to the donor (vmtvndn)</li>
                                                <li>a dwelling offered by a householder as a <b>kappiya kuṭi</b> 
                                                    <ul>
                                                        <li><b>note: </b>legality is not what counts here; if the donor offers it verbally, it is enough</li>
                                                    </ul>
                                                </li>
                                                <li>for bhikkhus: a vāsāgāra of <b>bhikkhunīs</b> </li>
                                                <li>for bhikkhunīs: a vāsāgāra of <b>bhikkhus</b> 
                                                    <ul>
                                                        <li><b>&quot;Saṅghikaṃ vā puggalikaṃ vā&quot;ti idaṃ kiñcāpi bhikkhunīnaṃ sāmaññato vuttaṃ,  bhikkhūnaṃ pana saṅghikaṃ puggalikañca bhikkhunīnaṃ, tāsaṃ saṅghikaṃ puggalikañca  bhikkhūnaṃ gihisantakaṭṭhāne tiṭṭhatīti veditabbaṃ. (Vmv-ṭ. II. 190.).   </b> </li>
                                                    </ul>
                                                </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                        <li><b>sammuti: </b>a building that was made as a kappiyakuṭi through a vinayakamma</li>
                                        <li><b>extra notes:</b>
                                            <ul>
                                                <li>kuṭi offering
                                                    <ul>
                                                        <li><b>&quot;yathā sukhaṁ paribhuñjati&quot;</b>‒can be used according to one&#39;s wish 
                                                            <ul>
                                                                <li>kuṭis in iit were offered with yathāsukhaṁ paribhuñjati</li>
                                                            </ul>
                                                        </li>
                                                        <li>the intention of the donor is what counts during the donation, if they say &quot;saṅghassa dema&quot; but they intend it to be, say,  bhikkhunī saṅgha&#39;s, it is so</li>
                                                    </ul>
                                                </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li><b>extra notes</b>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>related story: V. Karavikatissa&#39;s advice
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>it seems that, according to the story, if it is antovuttha and kept outside the kuṭi, it would become allowable, but this is not discussed</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li>sannidhikāraka<mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b> in the bowl</b></mark><mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"> </mark> 
                    <ul>
                        <li>if remnants of the previous day&#39;s food are mixed with the following day&#39;s food and consumed: <mark remnoteMark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;">{{8635080327922433::<b>pc for every swallow</b>}}</mark> 
                            <ul>
                                <li><b>why </b>is it an offense, if the food was pariccatta on the previous day?
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>ancient gaṇṭhipadas: it doesn&#39;t lose the paṭiggahaṇa; it is not properly relinquished, so it becomes sannidhi
                                            <ul>
                                                <li><b>Yadi evaṃ &quot;patto duddhoto hotī&quot;tiādīsu kasmā āpatti vuttāti? &quot;paṭiggahaṇaṃ avissajjetvāva  sayaṃ vā aññena vā tucchaṃ katvā na sammā dhovitvā niṭṭhāpite patte laggampi  avijahitapaṭiggahaṇameva hotīti tattha āpattī&quot;ti tīsupi gaṇṭhipadesu vuttaṃ. (Srd-ṭ. III. 67.).  </b> </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>standards for <b>washed bowls</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li>after washing it, one should fill it with clean water and look for oil slicks, or draw lines in the inner surface with the finger to check for oil lines</li>
                                <li><b>telavaṇṇa</b>patta (bowl with the color of oil): naturally shows lines when touched with the finger‒not taken into consideration</li>
                                <li><b>non-telavaṇṇa</b>patta: lines appear when rubbing a finger on the bowl&#39;s surface (not properly washed)</li>
                                <li><b>gaṇṭhika</b>patta (mended): sineha (oil) goes inside the gaṇṭhika (mendings), oozes out when heated,  and gets mixed with the food and is visible (not properly washed)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
